---
layout: article
title: Give every customer their own backend
description: Run one Appwrite project per customer, deploy a single app into all of them, serve it from your own domain, and bill each customer from their project's usage.
---

Your customers sign up for your product and never create an Appwrite account or open the Console.

Each customer still gets a backend of their own, with separate users, files, and database, in the region they picked. In this guide you build a platform that creates one project per customer and serves it under your own brand.

# The product you are building {% #product %}

{% only_dark %}
![The Studyhall operations console listing three schools, each with its own address, region, and project](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/operator-schools.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Studyhall operations console listing three schools, each with its own address, region, and project](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/operator-schools.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Studyhall sells a course platform to schools and training businesses. A school signs up, gets an address like `northgate.studyhall.app`, and starts enrolling learners. Their courses, handouts, and quizzes are written from the operations console, and their learners read and sit them in the classroom. Every row and file stays inside that school's project.

The platform is two apps. Studyhall operations is the console the Studyhall team uses, and the only place the Partners key lives. Classroom is what a school's learners see, built once and deployed into every school's project.

Every school gets its own Appwrite project. One project per school settles four things before you write any code:

- One school's learners cannot appear in another school's user list, because they are not in the same project.
- A school in Frankfurt and a school in Sydney can each hold their data where they need it.
- Appwrite counts usage per project, so billing a school means reading a number instead of building your own meter.
- When a school leaves, deleting their project removes their learners, rows, files, site, and domain rule together.

{% info title="Source code" %}
Both apps are on GitHub: [studyhall-operator](https://github.com/appwrite-community/studyhall-operator) for the console, and [studyhall-classroom](https://github.com/appwrite-community/studyhall-classroom) for the app each school runs. Clone them to read the working code alongside these steps.
{% /info %}

# One credential for the platform {% #credential %}

{% only_dark %}
![The Partners key dialog with the four scopes Studyhall needs selected](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/console-partners-key.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The Partners key dialog with the four scopes Studyhall needs selected](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/console-partners-key.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Studyhall holds one Appwrite credential: a [Partners key](/docs/partners/org-api-keys), created in the organization's settings under **Partners**. The key works at organization level. It can list, create, and delete projects, and issue keys for them. It cannot read or write data inside a project.

Four scopes cover the whole platform:

- `projects.read` lists the projects in the organization.
- `projects.write` creates, renames, and deletes a school's project.
- `organization.projects.keys.read` lists the keys a project holds.
- `organization.projects.keys.write` issues and revokes keys for a project.

Point the client at the organization rather than a project. The key belongs to one organization and cannot act on another.

```server-nodejs
import { Client, Organization } from 'node-appwrite'

export function organizationClient() {
  return new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://fra.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setOrganization(process.env.APPWRITE_ORGANIZATION_ID)
    .setKey(process.env.APPWRITE_PARTNERS_KEY)
}

export function organization() {
  return new Organization(organizationClient())
}
```

Keep it on the server, in a secrets manager, with one key per environment. A key holding `projects.write` can also delete a customer's project, so treat it as a production secret.

# Open a school {% #open %}

{% only_dark %}
![The add a school form, asking for a name, an address, a region, and a color](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/operator-add-school.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The add a school form, asking for a name, an address, a region, and a color](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/operator-add-school.avif)
{% /only_light %}

{% only_dark %}
![The organization's project list in the Console, one project per school](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/console-projects.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The organization's project list in the Console, one project per school](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/console-projects.avif)
{% /only_light %}

A school signs up and Studyhall creates their project. You choose a project's region at creation and cannot change it afterwards, so collect the region on your signup form.

```server-nodejs
const project = await organization().createProject({
  projectId: ID.unique(),
  name: tenant.name,
  region: tenant.region,
})
```

Store the returned ID on your own customer record. Every later call in this guide takes that project ID.

# Borrow a key instead of keeping one {% #keys %}

{% only_dark %}
![A course in the operations console, with its lessons, its quiz, and the school's handouts](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/operator-course.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![A course in the operations console, with its lessons, its quiz, and the school's handouts](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/operator-course.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Studyhall has to work inside the project it just created. Creating a long-lived key and storing it works, but it leaves your database holding one non-expiring credential per customer, each of which opens that customer's entire backend.

Ask for a key when a job starts instead. An ephemeral project key carries only the scopes you name and expires after the `duration` you set, up to 3600 seconds, so there is no stored credential to revoke later.

```server-nodejs
export async function borrowKey(projectId, scopes, seconds = 300) {
  const key = await new Organization(organizationClient()).createEphemeralProjectKey({
    projectId,
    scopes,
    duration: seconds,
  })
  return key.secret
}

export function projectClient(projectId, key) {
  return new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://fra.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject(projectId)
    .setKey(key)
}
```

Name the scopes per job rather than per customer. The key that uploads a handout gets `rows.write` and `files.write`, and cannot read anyone's quiz results:

```server-nodejs
export const SETUP_SCOPES = [
  'platforms.write',
  'databases.write',
  'tables.write',
  'buckets.write',
  'sites.read',
  'sites.write',
  'rules.write',
]

export const CONTENT_SCOPES = ['rows.read', 'rows.write']
export const MATERIAL_SCOPES = ['rows.read', 'rows.write', 'files.read', 'files.write']
export const ENROLMENT_SCOPES = ['users.read', 'users.write']
export const USAGE_SCOPES = ['usage.read']
```

Writing a course, a lesson, or a quiz question is one of those jobs, and it never touches storage, so `CONTENT_SCOPES` is enough. Uploading a handout or a recorded session needs both: the file goes into that school's bucket, and a row points the course at it.

```server-nodejs
const key = await borrowKey(tenant.projectId, MATERIAL_SCOPES, 300)
const client = projectClient(tenant.projectId, key)

const uploaded = await new Storage(client).createFile({
  bucketId: 'materials',
  fileId: ID.unique(),
  file: InputFile.fromBuffer(Buffer.from(await file.arrayBuffer()), file.name),
})

await new TablesDB(client).createRow({
  databaseId: 'classroom',
  tableId: 'materials',
  rowId: ID.unique(),
  data: {
    courseId,
    title,
    fileId: uploaded.$id,
    filename: file.name,
    mimeType: uploaded.mimeType,
    sizeBytes: uploaded.sizeOriginal,
  },
})
```

The bytes land in the school's own project, which is what makes them the school's storage to be billed for later.

# Set up their backend {% #setup %}

With a borrowed key, provisioning is normal project work. Register the school's hostname as a web platform first. Without that platform, Appwrite rejects the requests the classroom sends from a learner's browser:

```server-nodejs
const key = await borrowKey(project.$id, SETUP_SCOPES, 300)
const client = projectClient(project.$id, key)

await new Project(client).createWebPlatform({
  platformId: ID.unique(),
  name: tenant.name,
  hostname: `${tenant.slug}.studyhall.app`,
})
```

Then create the database and tables every school gets. Anyone signed in to that school can read courses, lessons, and handouts:

```server-nodejs
const db = new TablesDB(client)
const readable = [Permission.read(Role.users())]

await db.create({ databaseId: 'classroom', name: 'Classroom' })
await db.createTable({
  databaseId: 'classroom',
  tableId: 'courses',
  name: 'Courses',
  permissions: readable,
  rowSecurity: false,
})
```

Quiz questions are the exception. They carry the correct answer, so their table grants no read permission. The classroom writes each result with a permission naming the learner who sat the quiz:

```server-nodejs
await db.createTable({
  databaseId: 'classroom',
  tableId: 'questions',
  name: 'Questions',
  permissions: [],
  rowSecurity: false,
})

await db.createTable({
  databaseId: 'classroom',
  tableId: 'attempts',
  name: 'Attempts',
  permissions: [],
  rowSecurity: true,
})
```

Finally a bucket for everything a school uploads. Handouts are small, and a recorded session is not, so size the limit for the largest thing you accept rather than the typical one:

```server-nodejs
await new Storage(client).createBucket({
  bucketId: 'materials',
  name: 'Course materials',
  permissions: [],
  fileSecurity: false,
  maximumFileSize: 512 * 1000 * 1000,
})
```

# Ship your app into their project {% #ship %}

{% only_dark %}
![A school's site in the Console, built from the studyhall-classroom repository and serving their address](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/console-site.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![A school's site in the Console, built from the studyhall-classroom repository and serving their address](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/console-site.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Studyhall keeps one classroom app in one repository. Appwrite builds and hosts it inside each school's project, so you do not operate a server per school.

Create the site and declare the scopes its server code may use:

```server-nodejs
const sites = new Sites(client)

const site = await sites.create({
  siteId: ID.unique(),
  name: 'Classroom',
  framework: 'tanstack-start',
  adapter: 'ssr',
  buildRuntime: 'node-22',
  installCommand: 'npm install',
  buildCommand: 'npm run build',
  outputDirectory: './dist',
  scopes: ['sessions.write', 'users.read', 'rows.read', 'rows.write', 'files.read'],
})
```

Environment variables give each school its own name, accent color, and tagline, so one commit renders Northgate's classroom in green and Bright Minds' in blue:

```server-nodejs
for (const [key, value] of Object.entries({
  CLASSROOM_SCHOOL_NAME: tenant.name,
  CLASSROOM_ACCENT: tenant.accent,
  CLASSROOM_TAGLINE: 'Course materials and assessments',
})) {
  await sites.createVariable({ siteId: site.$id, variableId: ID.unique(), key, value })
}
```

Then deploy from the repository, pinned to a release tag:

```server-nodejs
await sites.createTemplateDeployment({
  siteId: site.$id,
  owner: 'appwrite-community',
  repository: 'studyhall-classroom',
  rootDirectory: './',
  type: 'tag',
  reference: 'v1.1.0',
  activate: true,
})
```

The repository is public, so Appwrite needs no Git connection to read it. Appwrite clones the repository, runs the install and build commands, and serves the result. A private repository takes the same path once you [connect the repository](/docs/products/sites/deploy-from-git).

Building is the slow part of opening a school. The project, platform, database, and site calls all return immediately, while the first deployment build takes about a minute. Show the school a setup screen until the deployment is ready.

# The credential the classroom never stores {% #site-key %}

{% only_dark %}
![A course in the classroom, with a recorded session, lessons, handouts, and the assessment](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/classroom-course.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![A course in the classroom, with a recorded session, lessons, handouts, and the assessment](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/classroom-course.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The classroom app reads courses, marks quizzes, and serves files. Each of those needs a server credential. The classroom app stores none.

Appwrite gives every request to a site a short-lived key in the `x-appwrite-key` header, limited to the `scopes` the site was created with. The app reads the header and uses it for the request it arrived on:

```server-nodejs
import { Client, TablesDB } from 'node-appwrite'
import { getRequestHeader } from '@tanstack/react-start/server'

function serverClient() {
  return new Client()
    .setEndpoint(process.env.APPWRITE_FUNCTION_API_ENDPOINT)
    .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_FUNCTION_PROJECT_ID)
    .setKey(getRequestHeader('x-appwrite-key'))
}
```

The app stores no credential, so there is nothing to rotate and nothing to leak from an environment file. The site's `scopes` are the limit of what the app can do, and each school's site holds its own copy. To let the classroom do more, call `updateSite` on every school's site, the same loop a release uses.

{% only_dark %}
![A quiz in the classroom, showing prompts and options only](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/classroom-quiz.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![A quiz in the classroom, showing prompts and options only](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/classroom-quiz.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The same request key serves files. Handouts download, and a recorded session plays in the page, which means the classroom has to answer a range request rather than send 30 MB every time a learner drags the scrubber:

```server-nodejs
const bytes = await new Storage(serverClient()).getFileDownload({ bucketId: 'materials', fileId })
const range = request.headers.get('range')

if (playable && range) {
  const match = /bytes=(\d*)-(\d*)/.exec(range)
  const start = match?.[1] ? Number(match[1]) : 0
  const end = match?.[2] ? Number(match[2]) : bytes.length - 1
  const chunk = bytes.subarray(start, end + 1)

  return new Response(chunk, {
    status: 206,
    headers: {
      'content-type': file.mimeType,
      'content-range': `bytes ${start}-${end}/${bytes.length}`,
      'accept-ranges': 'bytes',
    },
  })
}
```

Marking runs on the server. The browser receives prompts and options, never the correct answer:

```server-nodejs
const questions = await new TablesDB(serverClient()).listRows({
  databaseId: 'classroom',
  tableId: 'questions',
  queries: [Query.equal('quizId', quizId), Query.limit(100)],
})

const score = questions.rows.reduce(
  (total, question) => (answers[question.$id] === question.correctIndex ? total + 1 : total),
  0,
)
```

# Put it on their address {% #address %}

{% only_dark %}
![The school's domain attached to their classroom site](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/console-domain.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![The school's domain attached to their classroom site](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/console-domain.avif)
{% /only_light %}

A school reaches their classroom at `northgate.studyhall.app`. One rule points that hostname at their site:

```server-nodejs
const rule = await new Proxy(client).createSiteRule({
  domain: `${tenant.slug}.studyhall.app`,
  siteId: site.$id,
})
```

Appwrite checks DNS while it creates the rule. If the hostname resolves, Appwrite issues a certificate for it. If it does not, the rule stays unverified and Appwrite records the reason in the rule's logs. Call `updateRuleStatus` to check again once the record has propagated.

{% info title="One DNS record, once" %}
Point `*.studyhall.app` at `appwrite.network` with a CNAME at your DNS provider. Every school's hostname then resolves and verifies on its own. A Partners key cannot create DNS records, so add this record yourself before you onboard the first school.

Claim the domain in your organization's **Domains** settings as well. Rules are unique by hostname across Appwrite, and claiming the name stops another organization from creating a rule on a subdomain of yours.
{% /info %}

# Bill for what they used {% #billing %}

{% only_dark %}
![A school's page in the operations console, showing active learners, material held, and the month's total](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/dark/operator-school.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![A school's page in the operations console, showing active learners, material held, and the month's total](/images/docs/partners/guides/white-label/operator-school.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Studyhall charges a school for the learners who signed in during the month and for the files the school stores. Appwrite measures both per project.

```server-nodejs
const gauges = await new Usage(projectClient(tenant.projectId, key)).listGauges({
  metrics: ['users.mau', 'files.storage'],
})

const latest = (metric) => {
  const points = gauges.metrics.find((entry) => entry.metric === metric)?.points ?? []
  return points.length ? Number(points[points.length - 1].value) : 0
}

const learners = latest('users.mau')
const storageBytes = latest('files.storage')
```

`users.mau` is the number of learners with a session in the current month, and `files.storage` counts the files in the school's buckets. Recordings are what move that number. A single 31 MB session accounts for nearly all of Northgate's 35 MB, and their four PDFs come to under 3 MB together. `deployments.storage` and `builds.storage` measure your own build output, so leave them out when you bill only for what the school stores.

`files.storage` is a gauge. It reports the bytes held at the moment you read it rather than a total accumulated across the period, so read it when the billing period closes and bill that figure.

{% info title="Keep your own copy" %}
Usage history goes back 30 days. Appwrite rejects a `startAt` older than that with `limit_history_exceeded`, so you cannot recover a period you did not read at the time. Write each customer's counters into your own database at the close of every period, and bill from your copy.
{% /info %}

# Roll out and close the account {% #lifecycle %}

Every school runs the version Studyhall gave them, so an upgrade is a loop over schools. One that asked to stay on the previous release keeps its old tag:

```server-nodejs
export async function releaseTo(tenant, tag) {
  const key = await borrowKey(tenant.projectId, ['sites.read', 'sites.write'], 600)

  return new Sites(projectClient(tenant.projectId, key)).createTemplateDeployment({
    siteId: tenant.siteId,
    owner: 'appwrite-community',
    repository: 'studyhall-classroom',
    rootDirectory: './',
    type: 'tag',
    reference: tag,
    activate: true,
  })
}
```

To undo a deployment that went wrong, activate the previous one with `updateSiteDeployment`. It applies to that school and nobody else.

Deleting the project is one call. It removes the school's learners, courses, files, site, and domain rule.

```server-nodejs
await new Organization(organizationClient()).deleteProject({ projectId: tenant.projectId })
```

Remove your own record of the school afterwards, and tell them to drop the CNAME if it was theirs rather than a subdomain of yours.

